[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a complete, ready‑to‑copy solution that does the same thing on Android that `maplibregl.addProtocol()` does on the web.

### TL;DR
* **MapLibre Android** lets you plug a custom HTTP‑request handler into the native core.  
* Implement `org.maplibre.android.http.HttpRequestFactory` (and a tiny `HttpRequest`/`HttpResponse` pair) that recognises the `vault://` scheme, decrypts the tile from your blob and returns the raw bytes.  
* Register that factory **once**, before any `MapView`/`MapLibreMap` is created (normally in `Application.onCreate()` or the first Activity).  
* In your style JSON keep the URL exactly as you would on the web, e.g.  

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "my‑tiles": {
      "type": "vector",
      "tiles": ["vault://tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"]
    }
  },
  …
}
```

That’s all – the native renderer will call your code for every tile request that starts with `vault://`.

---

## 1. The pieces you need to implement

| Interface / class | What you have to do |
|-------------------|---------------------|
| `HttpRequestFactory` | Return a custom `HttpRequest` for URLs that start with `vault://`. For everything else you can delegate to the default factory (`DefaultHttpRequestFactory`). |
| `HttpRequest` | Perform the actual work: parse `{z}/{x}/{y}` from the URL, read the encrypted blob, decrypt it, and hand the raw bytes back. |
| `HttpResponse` | Simple wrapper that gives the native core a status code, optional headers and an `InputStream` containing the tile data. |

All three live in the **`org.maplibre.android.http`** package (the same package that the web SDK uses internally).

---

## 2. Full Kotlin implementation

```kotlin
package com.example.maplibrevault

import android.net.Uri
import org.maplibre.android.http.*
import java.io.ByteArrayInputStream
import java.io.InputStream

/**
 * 1️⃣  Factory that decides which request implementation to use.
 */
class VaultHttpRequestFactory(
    private val fallbackFactory: HttpRequestFactory = DefaultHttpRequestFactory()
) : HttpRequestFactory {

    override fun createRequest(url: String): HttpRequest {
        return if (url.startsWith("vault://")) {
            VaultHttpRequest(url)
        } else {
            // Let the SDK handle normal http/https URLs
            fallbackFactory.createRequest(url)
        }
    }
}

/**
 * 2️⃣  The request that will be executed for every “vault://” tile.
 */
private class VaultHttpRequest(
    private val url: String
) : HttpRequest {

    override fun execute(): HttpResponse {
        // ---- Parse z/x/y -------------------------------------------------
        // vault://tiles/12/3456/7890.pbf   →  ["tiles","12","3456","7890.pbf"]
        val uri = Uri.parse(url)
        val segments = uri.pathSegments
        if (segments.size < 4) {
            return errorResponse(400, "Malformed vault URL")
        }

        val z = segments[1].toInt()
        val x = segments[2].toInt()
        // the y part may contain an extension (".pbf") – strip it
        val yPart = segments[3]
        val y = yPart.substringBefore('.').toInt()

        // ---- Load & decrypt ------------------------------------------------
        // You will replace this with your own storage / decryption logic.
        val encrypted = loadEncryptedTileFromBlob(z, x, y)
        val plain = decryptTile(encrypted)

        // ---- Return a successful HttpResponse -----------------------------
        return object : HttpResponse {
            override fun getStatusCode(): Int = 200
            override fun getHeaders(): Map<String, List<String>> = emptyMap()
            override fun getBody(): InputStream = ByteArrayInputStream(plain)
        }
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Helper methods – replace with your real implementation
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    private fun loadEncryptedTileFromBlob(z: Int, x: Int, y: Int): ByteArray {
        // Example: read from a file that contains a concatenated blob.
        // In a real app you probably have an indexed SQLite DB or a
        // custom binary format.
        // throw IllegalStateException("Implement your storage read here")
        TODO("Read the encrypted tile bytes for ($z,$x,$y) from your blob")
    }

    private fun decryptTile(encrypted: ByteArray): ByteArray {
        // Example: AES‑GCM with a key stored in the Android Keystore.
        // throw IllegalStateException("Implement your decryption here")
        TODO("Decrypt the tile bytes and return the raw .pbf")
    }

    private fun errorResponse(code: Int, message: String): HttpResponse =
        object : HttpResponse {
            override fun getStatusCode(): Int = code
            override fun getHeaders(): Map<String, List<String>> = emptyMap()
            override fun getBody(): InputStream =
                ByteArrayInputStream(message.toByteArray(Charsets.UTF_8))
        }
}

/**
 * 3️⃣  Register the factory **once** before any MapLibre objects are created.
 *
 * Put this in your Application class (or the first Activity before you
 * inflate a MapView).
 */
class MyApp : android.app.Application() {
    override fun onCreate() {
        super.onCreate()

        // Build the MapLibre options and inject our factory
        val options = org.maplibre.android.MapLibreOptions()
        options.httpRequestFactory = VaultHttpRequestFactory()

        // Initialise the SDK with the custom options
        org.maplibre.android.MapLibre.getInstance(this, options)
    }
}
```

### What the code does

1. **Factory** – `VaultHttpRequestFactory` checks the URL. If it starts with `vault://` it returns our custom request, otherwise it hands the URL to the default factory (so normal HTTP/HTTPS sources keep working).
2. **Request** – `VaultHttpRequest.execute()` parses the `{z}/{x}/{y}` placeholders, reads the encrypted tile from wherever you store it, decrypts it, and wraps the raw bytes in an `HttpResponse`.
3. **Response** – The native core only cares about the status code and an `InputStream`. No extra headers are needed for vector tiles.
4. **Registration** – `MapLibre.getInstance(context, options)` must be called **before** any `MapView` is inflated. The SDK stores the factory globally and passes it to the C++ core via JNI.

---

## 3. How it maps to the web `addProtocol` call

| Web (`maplibregl.addProtocol`) | Android (MapLibre) |
|--------------------------------|--------------------|
| `addProtocol('vault', handler)` | `HttpRequestFactory` that returns a custom `HttpRequest` for URLs whose scheme is `vault` |
| `handler(parameters, callback)` | `HttpRequest.execute()` → returns an `HttpResponse` (status + InputStream) |
| `callback(null, arrayBuffer)`   | `HttpResponse.getBody()` supplies the same `arrayBuffer` as an `InputStream` |

The only difference is that on Android the “callback” is synchronous – the native core calls `execute()` on a background thread and expects the response immediately. If your decryption is CPU‑heavy you may want to off‑load it to a worker thread **inside** `execute()`, but keep the method blocking until you have the bytes (the SDK already runs it off the UI thread).

---

## 4. Things to keep in mind

| Issue | What to do |
|-------|------------|
| **Threading** | `HttpRequest.execute()` is called on a thread from the SDK’s internal pool, not on the UI thread. You can safely do I/O and crypto here. |
| **Caching** | The SDK will cache the response according to the HTTP cache‑control headers you return. If you want to use MapLibre’s built‑in tile cache, simply add a `Cache-Control: max‑age=…` header in the `HttpResponse`. |
| **Error handling** | Return a non‑200 status (e.g. 404) if the tile cannot be found or decryption fails. The map will render a transparent tile and will retry according to the SDK’s retry policy. |

Below is the “Android‑side” equivalent of the **`maplibregl.addProtocol()`** you use on the web.

### TL;DR
MapLibre‑Android lets you plug a handler for any URL scheme through **`FileSource.setCustomProtocolHandler()`**.  
Register a handler for the `vault://` scheme, decrypt the tile you need and return the raw bytes (or an error) via the `FileSource.Callback`. After that you can keep the same style JSON you use on the web – just point the vector source to `vault://tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}`.

---

## 1. What you need to know

| Web (MapLibre‑GL‑JS) | Android (MapLibre‑Native) |
|----------------------|---------------------------|
| `maplibregl.addProtocol('vault', handler)` | `FileSource.getInstance(context).setCustomProtocolHandler("vault", handler)` |
| Handler receives the full URL and must call `callback(null, data)` (or reject) | Handler receives the URL and a `FileSource.Callback`. You call `callback.onSuccess(Response(...))` or `callback.onError(message)` |
| Returns a `Uint8Array` (or a Blob) | Returns a `FileSource.Response` that contains a `ByteArray` and an optional MIME type |
| Works for any source type (raster, vector, glyphs, etc.) | Works for any source that uses a URL (vector tiles, raster tiles, glyphs, sprites, …) |

> **Why `FileSource`?**  
> `FileSource` is the low‑level I/O layer used by the native SDK for *all* network and offline resources (tiles, glyphs, sprites, style JSON, …). By installing a custom protocol handler you intercept the request **before** the SDK tries to go to the network, exactly like `addProtocol` does in the browser.

---

## 2. Full Kotlin example

Below is a self‑contained example that

1. Registers a `vault://` protocol handler.
2. Parses the `{z}/{x}/{y}` placeholders.
3. Looks up the encrypted blob on the device (you replace the stub with your own storage/decryption logic).
4. Returns the decrypted MVT bytes to the SDK.

```kotlin
package com.example.maplibrevault

import android.content.Context
import android.net.Uri
import android.util.Log
import com.mapbox.mapboxsdk.maps.Style
import com.mapbox.mapboxsdk.maps.MapView
import com.mapbox.mapboxsdk.maps.MapboxMap
import com.mapbox.mapboxsdk.storage.FileSource
import com.mapbox.mapboxsdk.storage.FileSource.Response
import java.io.File
import java.io.IOException

/**
 * Call this **once**, before you load any style that contains a `vault://` URL.
 */
fun registerVaultProtocol(context: Context) {
    // Get the singleton FileSource used by the SDK
    val fileSource = FileSource.getInstance(context)

    // Register a handler for the scheme "vault"
    fileSource.setCustomProtocolHandler("vault") { url: String, callback: FileSource.Callback ->

        try {
            // --------------------------------------------------------------
            // 1️⃣ Parse the URL – we expect: vault://tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}
            // --------------------------------------------------------------
            val uri = Uri.parse(url)
            // Path segments after the authority (tiles) are: [z, x, y]
            val pathSegments = uri.pathSegments
            if (pathSegments.size < 3) {
                callback.onError("Invalid vault URL – not enough path segments: $url")
                return@setCustomProtocolHandler
            }

            val z = pathSegments[0].toInt()
            val x = pathSegments[1].toInt()
            val y = pathSegments[2].toInt()

            // --------------------------------------------------------------
            // 2️⃣ Locate the encrypted blob and decrypt it.
            // --------------------------------------------------------------
            // This is where you plug your own storage / decryption code.
            // For the demo we just read a file from the app's private dir.
            val tileBytes = decryptTileFromVault(context, z, x, y)

            // --------------------------------------------------------------
            // 3️⃣ Hand the bytes back to the SDK.
            // --------------------------------------------------------------
            // The MIME type for Mapbox Vector Tiles is "application/x-protobuf"
            val response = Response(tileBytes, "application/x-protobuf")
            callback.onSuccess(response)

        } catch (e: Exception) {
            // Anything that goes wrong should be reported via onError()
            Log.e("VaultProtocol", "Failed to load $url", e)
            callback.onError(e.message ?: "Unknown error")
        }
    }
}

/**
 * Stub – replace with your real decryption / storage logic.
 *
 * In this example we assume each tile is stored as an encrypted file:
 *   <app‑files>/vault/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.mvt.enc
 *
 * The function reads the file, decrypts it (AES‑GCM in this case) and
 * returns the raw MVT bytes.
 */
private fun decryptTileFromVault(context: Context, z: Int, x: Int, y: Int): ByteArray {
    // Build the path to the encrypted file
    val encryptedFile = File(
        context.filesDir,
        "vault/tiles/$z/$x/$y.mvt.enc"
    )

    if (!encryptedFile.isFile) {
        throw IOException("Encrypted tile not found: $encryptedFile")
    }

    // Read the encrypted blob
    val encryptedBytes = encryptedFile.readBytes()

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Your decryption routine goes here.
    // For illustration we just return the bytes unchanged.
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // Example (AES‑GCM):
    // val cipher = Cipher.getInstance("AES/GCM/NoPadding")
    // val keySpec = SecretKeySpec(myKeyBytes, "AES")
    // val iv = encryptedBytes.sliceArray(0 until 12)          // first 12 bytes = IV
    // val tag = encryptedBytes.sliceArray(encryptedBytes.size - 16 until encryptedBytes.size)
    // val cipherText = encryptedBytes.sliceArray(12 until encryptedBytes.size - 16)
    // val gcmSpec = GCMParameterSpec(128, iv)
    // cipher.init(Cipher.DECRYPT_MODE, keySpec, gcmSpec)
    // return cipher.doFinal(cipherText + tag)
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------

    // **NOTE:** If you keep the tiles un‑encrypted for testing, just return them:
    return encryptedBytes
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   How to use it in an Activity / Fragment
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
class MapActivity : AppCompatActivity() {

    private lateinit var mapView: MapView
    private var mapboxMap: MapboxMap? = null

    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)

        // 1️⃣ Register the protocol **before** any MapView is created
        registerVaultProtocol(this)

        // 2️⃣ Inflate layout that contains a MapView (or create it programmatically)
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_map)
        mapView = findViewById(R.id.mapView)
        mapView.onCreate(savedInstanceState)

        mapView.getMapAsync { mapboxMap ->
            this.mapboxMap = mapboxMap

            // 3️⃣ Load a style that references the vault scheme
            mapboxMap.setStyle(
                Style.Builder()
                    .fromUri("asset://my_style.json")   // <-- style contains "vault://tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}"
            ) { style ->
                // style is ready – you can add sources / layers programmatically if you want
            }
        }
    }

    // ---- lifecycle forwarding -------------------------------------------------
    override fun onStart() = super.onStart().also { mapView.onStart() }
    override fun onResume() = super.onResume().

## TL;DR  
On Android the MapLibre‑GL‑Native SDK does **not** have a `addProtocol` method like the web version.  
Instead you plug a **global `HttpRequestHandler`** into the SDK.  
The handler is called for **every** URL the style engine tries to fetch – raster tiles, vector tiles, glyphs, sprites, style JSON, etc. – and you can recognise your own scheme (`vault://…`) and return the decrypted bytes yourself.

Below is a complete, ready‑to‑copy Kotlin example that shows:

1. **Create a custom `HttpRequestHandler`.**  
2. **Register it** with `MapLibre.getInstance(context)`.  
3. **Reference the custom scheme** in your style JSON (`"url": "vault://tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"`).  

---

## 1. The “protocol‑hook” in MapLibre Android

```kotlin
import android.content.Context
import android.net.Uri
import org.maplibre.android.MapLibre
import org.maplibre.android.http.HttpRequestHandler
import org.maplibre.android.http.Request
import org.maplibre.android.http.Response

/**
 * A handler that knows how to serve tiles that live in an encrypted blob.
 * All other requests are delegated to the default network handler.
 */
class VaultHttpRequestHandler(
    private val context: Context,
    private val vault: TileVault   // your own class that knows how to decrypt
) : HttpRequestHandler {

    // The default handler that MapLibre ships with (uses OkHttp under the hood)
    private val defaultHandler = HttpRequestHandler.DEFAULT

    override fun handleRequest(request: Request): Response {
        val uri = Uri.parse(request.url)

        // --------------------------------------------------------------
        // 1️⃣  Intercept only our custom scheme
        // --------------------------------------------------------------
        if (uri.scheme == "vault") {
            // Expected format: vault://tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}
            // (MapLibre will already have replaced {z}/{x}/{y} with numbers)
            val z = uri.pathSegments[1].toInt()
            val x = uri.pathSegments[2].toInt()
            val y = uri.pathSegments[3].toInt()

            // -----------------------------------------------------------------
            // 2️⃣  Pull the encrypted tile from your blob and decrypt it
            // -----------------------------------------------------------------
            val encrypted = vault.readEncryptedTile(z, x, y)   // ← your own I/O
            val plainBytes = vault.decrypt(encrypted)        // ← your own crypto

            // -----------------------------------------------------------------
            // 3️⃣  Return a successful Response with the raw PBF bytes
            // -----------------------------------------------------------------
            // The MIME type for Mapbox vector tiles is "application/x-protobuf"
            return Response.success(plainBytes, "application/x-protobuf")
        }

        // --------------------------------------------------------------
        // Anything else – let the built‑in handler do its job
        // --------------------------------------------------------------
        return defaultHandler.handleRequest(request)
    }
}
```

### What you need to provide

| Piece | What it does | Typical implementation |
|------|--------------|------------------------|
| `TileVault` (or any class you name) | Reads the encrypted blob from storage and decrypts it. | Could be a simple wrapper around `EncryptedSharedPreferences`, a SQLCipher DB, a file on the internal storage, etc. |
| `readEncryptedTile(z, x, y)` | Returns the raw encrypted byte array for the requested tile. | Use your own indexing scheme (e.g., `z/x/y.pbf.enc`). |
| `decrypt(encrypted)` | Returns the clear‑text MVT bytes. | AES‑GCM, ChaCha20‑Poly1305, … whatever you already use on the web side. |

---

## 2. Register the handler **once** (before any `MapView` is created)

```kotlin
// Usually in your Application class or the first Activity that creates a map
class MyApplication : Application() {

    override fun onCreate() {
        super.onCreate()

        // Initialise the MapLibre SDK (you still need to call this)
        MapLibre.getInstance(this)

        // Create your vault helper (inject dependencies, keys, etc.)
        val vault = TileVault(this)   // <-- your own implementation

        // Plug the handler into the SDK
        MapLibre.getInstance(this).setHttpRequestHandler(
            VaultHttpRequestHandler(this, vault)
        )
    }
}
```

> **Important:**  
> *The handler must be set **before** the first `MapView`/`MapLibreMap` is instantiated.*  
> If you set it later the first style load will already have tried (and failed) to fetch the tiles.

---

## 3. Use the custom scheme in your style JSON

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "my-vault-tiles": {
      "type": "vector",
      "tiles": [
        "vault://tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"
      ],
      "minzoom": 0,
      "maxzoom": 14
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "water",
      "type": "fill",
      "source": "my-vault-tiles",
      "source-layer": "water",
      "paint": {
        "fill-color": "#a0c8f0"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

*The `{z}/{x}/{y}` placeholders are replaced by the SDK before the request is handed to the `HttpRequestHandler`.*

---

## 4. Full minimal example (Application + Activity)

```kotlin
// ---------- MyApplication.kt ----------
class MyApplication : Application() {
    override fun onCreate() {
        super.onCreate()
        MapLibre.getInstance(this)

        val vault = TileVault(this)   // your own class
        MapLibre.getInstance(this).setHttpRequestHandler(
            VaultHttpRequestHandler(this, vault)
        )
    }
}

// ---------- MainActivity.kt ----------
class MainActivity : AppCompatActivity() {

    private lateinit var mapView: MapView

    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)

        // Inflate a layout that contains a MapView with id "mapView"
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main)
        mapView = findViewById(R.id.mapView)

        mapView.getMapboxMap().loadStyle(
            style = Style.Builder().fromJson(loadStyleJsonFromAssets()),
            onStyleLoaded = {
                // map is ready – tiles will be fetched via your VaultHttpRequestHandler
            }
        )
    }

    private fun loadStyleJsonFromAssets(): String {
        assets.open("my_vault_style.json").bufferedReader().use {
            return it.readText()
        }
    }

    // Remember to forward lifecycle events to the MapView
    override fun onStart() = super.onStart().also { mapView.onStart() }
    override fun onStop() = super.onStop().also { mapView.onStop() }
    override fun onLowMemory() = super.onLowMemory().also { mapView.onLowMemory() }
    override fun onDestroy() = super.onDestroy().also { mapView.onDestroy() }
    override fun onResume() = super.onResume().also { mapView.onResume() }
    override fun onPause() = super.onPause().also { mapView.onPause() }
}
```

---

## 5. Gotchas & Tips

| Issue | How to avoid / fix |
|-------|--------------------|
| **`HttpRequestHandler` is called for *all* network traffic** (glyphs, sprites, style JSON, etc.) | Inside `handleRequest` check `uri.scheme`. If it isn’t `vault`, just delegate to `DEFAULT`. |
| **Large tiles → OOM** | Decrypt directly into a `ByteArray` that you return; do **not** keep a copy in a static cache unless you manage its size. |
| **Threading** | `handleRequest` is executed on a background thread (MapLibre’s internal thread pool). You can do I/O & crypto here safely. |
| **HTTPS / certificate pinning** | Not relevant for `vault://` because you never hit the network. For everything else you still get the default OkHttp handling (including TLS). |
| **Testing** | Use a simple style that points to a single tile (`vault://tiles/0/0/0.pbf`) and log the request URL to verify the handler is hit. |
| **Cache** | If you want offline caching *in addition* to your encrypted blob, you can still enable the built‑in `TileStore` – the handler will be called **after** the cache miss, so you can let the default handler fill the cache for normal HTTP URLs. |
| **Multiple handlers** | Only one global handler can be registered. If you need more complex routing, implement it inside a single handler (e.g., switch on `uri.scheme` or on a prefix). |

