## 7. Bottom line

* **Web:** `maplibregl.addProtocol('vault', handler)`  
* **Android (MapLibre‑GL‑Native):** implement `HttpRequestHandler` and register it with `MapLibre.getInstance(context).setHttpRequestHandler(yourHandler)`.  

That’s the exact analogue – it gives you full control over any URL the SDK tries to load, letting you decrypt your encrypted MVT blobs on‑the‑fly. Happy mapping!

## TL;DR  
The style JSON **does** contain the globe projection, but the iOS SDK does not apply it automatically.  
You have to tell **MapLibre‑GL‑Native for iOS** to use the globe after the style has finished loading (or via the map‑view options when you create the view).

```swift
import MapLibre

class MapViewController: UIViewController, MLNMapViewDelegate {

    var mapView: MLNMapView!

    override func viewDidLoad() {
        super.viewDidLoad()

        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        // 1️⃣  Create the view – you can also pass the projection in the
        //     options dictionary (available from v6.0+)
        // -----------------------------------------------------------------
        let styleURL = URL(string: "https://example.com/your‑style.json")!
        mapView = MLNMapView(frame: view.bounds, styleURL: styleURL)
        mapView.autoresizingMask = [.flexibleWidth, .flexibleHeight]
        mapView.delegate = self
        view.addSubview(mapView)

        // Optional – give the camera a little pitch so you can see the curvature
        var cam = mapView.camera
        cam.pitch = 45
        cam.zoom = 0          // zoom out enough to see the whole globe
        mapView.setCamera(cam, animated: false)
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣  When the style is ready, force the globe projection
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------
    func mapView(_ mapView: MLNMapView, didFinishLoading style: MLNStyle) {
        // The style JSON may already contain `"projection": {"type":"globe"}`.
        // The iOS SDK still starts in Mercator, so we set it explicitly.
        if #available(iOS 13.0, *) {
            style.projection = .globe          // ← the key line
        }

        // If you want to keep the style‑JSON‑only approach, you can also
        // re‑apply the style after setting the projection:
        // mapView.styleURL = mapView.styleURL
    }
}
```

### Why the extra step is needed
* **MapLibre‑GL‑Native for iOS** (the `MLNMapView` component) defaults to the Mercator projection, even when the style JSON contains a `"projection"` object.  
* The Android and Web SDKs automatically switch to the projection defined in the style, but the iOS SDK requires an explicit call (`style.projection = .globe`) after the style has been parsed.  
* This behaviour is documented in the iOS API (`MLNStyle.projection`) and is the same in the Mapbox‑GL‑Native iOS SDK from which MapLibre is forked.

### Alternative: set the projection when you create the view (MapLibre ≥ 6.0)

If you are on a recent MapLibre version (≥ 6.0), you can pass the projection in the initializer instead of waiting for the delegate callback:

```swift
let options = MLNMapViewOptions()
options.projection = .globe          // ← set globe up‑front

let mapView = MLNMapView(frame: view.bounds,
                         styleURL: styleURL,
                         options: options)
```

Both approaches achieve the same result.

### Things to keep in mind

| Issue | Fix / Note |
|-------|------------|
| **Globe not visible at high zoom** | The globe only becomes apparent when you zoom out far enough (zoom ≤ 1‑2). Set an initial low zoom or let the user zoom out. |
| **Flat appearance despite projection** | Make sure you set the projection **after** the style finishes loading (`didFinishLoading style:`). Doing it earlier (e.g. in `viewDidLoad`) will be overwritten when the style is parsed. |
| **Pitch = 0** | With a pitch of 0 the globe looks like a flat map. Add a modest pitch (30‑45°) to see the curvature. |
| **Older MapLibre version** | Globe support was added in the iOS SDK around v5.0. If you’re on an older version, upgrade via Swift Package Manager. |
| **Style JSON already contains `"projection"`** | You can keep it – the SDK will ignore it until you set `style.projection`. No conflict. |
| **Performance** | Globe rendering is a bit more GPU‑intensive. Test on older devices if you notice frame‑rate drops. |

### Quick checklist for a working globe

1. **Use a MapLibre version that includes globe support** (≥ 5.0, preferably the latest 6.x).  
2. **Add `"projection": {"type":"globe"}`** to your style JSON (optional but nice for consistency).  
3. **Create the `MLNMapView`** (or pass the projection via `MLNMapViewOptions`).  
4. **Implement `mapView(_:didFinishLoading:)`** and set `style.projection = .globe`.  
5. **Set a low initial zoom** and a non‑zero pitch if you want to see the curvature right away.  

That’s it – your MapLibre map on iOS will now render as a 3‑D globe just like it does in the browser. Happy mapping! 🚀

Below is a **complete, ready‑to‑paste** Kotlin snippet that does the same thing you do on the web:

```kotlin
import android.os.Bundle
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity
import com.mapbox.mapboxsdk.maps.MapView
import com.mapbox.mapboxsdk.maps.MapLibreMap
import com.mapbox.mapboxsdk.maps.Style
import com.mapbox.mapboxsdk.style.sources.RasterDemSource
import com.mapbox.mapboxsdk.style.layers.Terrain

class MainActivity : AppCompatActivity() {

    private lateinit var mapView: MapView

    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)

        // 1️⃣  Inflate a layout that contains a MapView (or create it programmatically)
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main)
        mapView = findViewById(R.id.mapView)

        // 2️⃣  Initialise the MapView
        mapView.onCreate(savedInstanceState)

        // 3️⃣  Get the MapLibreMap instance asynchronously
        mapView.getMapAsync { mapLibreMap: MapLibreMap ->

            // 4️⃣  Wait until the style is fully loaded before touching it
            mapLibreMap.getStyle { style: Style ->

                // --------------------------------------------------------------
                // 5️⃣  1️⃣ Add the raster‑DEM source (equivalent to the web call)
                // --------------------------------------------------------------
                val demSource = RasterDemSource(
                    "dem",                     // <-- source id (must match the one you’ll use later)
                    "https://example.com/terrain.json" // <-- URL to the TileJSON / .json descriptor
                )
                style.addSource(demSource)

                // --------------------------------------------------------------
                // 6️⃣  2️⃣ Enable terrain using that source and set an exaggeration
                // --------------------------------------------------------------
                val terrain = Terrain("dem")          // source id we just added
                terrain.exaggeration = 1.5            // same as {exaggeration: 1.5} in JS
                style.setTerrain(terrain)

                // --------------------------------------------------------------
                // 7️⃣  (Optional) – you can now add a hillshade layer, etc.
                // --------------------------------------------------------------
                // style.addLayerBelow(
                //     HillshadeLayer("hillshade", "dem"),
                //     "waterway-label"
                // )
            }
        }
    }

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Boiler‑plate lifecycle forwarding – required for MapView
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    override fun onStart()   = super.onStart().also { mapView.onStart() }
    override fun onResume()  = super.onResume().also { mapView.onResume() }
    override fun onPause()   = super.onPause().also { mapView.onPause() }
    override fun onStop()    = super.onStop().also { mapView.onStop() }
    override fun onLowMemory() = super.onLowMemory().also { mapView.onLowMemory() }
    override fun onDestroy() = super.onDestroy().also { mapView.onDestroy() }
    override fun onSaveInstanceState(outState: Bundle) {
        super.onSaveInstanceState(outState)
        mapView.onSaveInstanceState(outState)
    }
}
```

### What the code does, line‑by‑line

| Step | Web API | Android (MapLibre) equivalent |
|------|----------|--------------------------------|
| 1️⃣  | `map.addSource('dem', {type: 'raster-dem', url: '…'})` | `RasterDemSource("dem", "https://example.com/terrain.json")` then `style.addSource(demSource)` |
| 2️⃣  | `map.setTerrain({source: 'dem', exaggeration: 1.5})` | `Terrain("dem").apply { exaggeration = 1.5 }` then `style.setTerrain(terrain)` |

### Important notes

| Topic | Details |
|-------|---------|
| **When to run the code** | You must wait for the style to be loaded (`mapLibreMap.getStyle { … }`). Trying to add a source or terrain before the style is ready will silently fail. |
| **Source ID** | The string `"dem"` is the *source identifier*. It must be the same in `RasterDemSource` and `Terrain`. |
| **URL format** | The URL must point to a **TileJSON** document that describes the raster‑DEM tiles (or a direct `.json` descriptor that MapLibre can interpret). The same URL you used on the web works here. |
| **Exaggeration** | `Terrain.exaggeration` is a `Double`. The default is `1.0`. Setting it to `1.5` gives the same visual effect as the JS `exaggeration: 1.5`. |
| **Dependencies** | Make sure you have the MapLibre Android SDK in your `build.gradle`:<br>`implementation "org.maplibre.gl:android-sdk:10.5.0"` (or the latest version). |
| **Optional hillshade** | If you later want a hillshade layer you can add it exactly like the commented block – just remember to reference the same DEM source id (`"dem"`). |
| **Threading** | All style manipulation must happen on the **main UI thread** (the callbacks above already run there). |

