## TL;DR  

You **don’t have to run a tile server or convert the file to MBTiles**.  
MapLibre iOS can read a `.pmtiles` archive directly – you just need a tiny bridge that:

1. **Downloads (or streams) the archive** from Cloudflare R2 using HTTP Range requests (the same way the `pmtiles://` protocol does in the browser).  
2. **Exposes the tiles to MapLibre** through a custom `TileDataSource` (or a `URLProtocol` that handles the `pmtiles://` scheme).

The easiest way to do that today is to add the **official Swift PMTiles library** (`pmtiles-swift`) to your project and wrap it in a `TileDataSource`.  
Below is a step‑by‑step guide, a minimal implementation, and a few “gotchas” to keep in mind.

---

## 1. What’s the landscape?

| Option | What it means | Pros | Cons |
|--------|----------------|------|------|
| **Run a tile server** (e.g. TileServer‑GL, Tileserver‑PHP) | Serve individual PNG/Vector tiles over HTTP | Works with any MapLibre client, easy to debug | Extra infrastructure, latency, cost |
| **Convert to MBTiles** | Turn the PMTiles file into an SQLite‑based MBTiles file | Native support in MapLibre iOS (via `TileStore`) | Requires a one‑off conversion, doubles the file size, loses the streaming‑‑only‑when‑needed advantage of PMTiles |
| **Write a custom source** (recommended) | Use the PMTiles spec directly from the archive (remote or local) | No extra server, no conversion, works with a 3 GB archive on R2, uses HTTP range requests, fully offline‑capable if you cache the file | Small amount of Swift glue code required |

Because PMTiles was designed to be **readable over HTTP range requests**, the “custom source” path gives you the same behaviour you already have on the web (the `pmtiles://` protocol handler) without any extra moving parts.

---

## 2. The Swift PMTiles library

The MapLibre team ships a **first‑class Swift implementation**:

```
https://github.com/maplibre/pmtiles-swift
```

* It can open a remote archive (`https://…/mytiles.pmtiles`) **or** a local file (`file:///…/mytiles.pmtiles`).  
* It implements the full PMTiles v3 spec (including the new “tile‑by‑hash” layout).  
* It is distributed as a **Swift Package Manager** (SPM) package, so adding it to an Xcode project is a one‑click operation.

> **TL;DR** – Add the package to your project (File → Add Packages → paste the URL).  

